      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.data4thepeople.com/p/lorenz-chart-viz#faq",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does this include business owners, the self-employed or investment income?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The chart covers payroll (W-2) wages only. It leaves out business owners, the self-employed, and income from dividends, interest, rent and capital gains. That is where most of the very highest incomes are: on 2023 tax returns reporting $10 million or more, wages were 17.0% of total income. So this chart cannot tell you whether overall income inequality rose or fell. The \"What this chart can't see\" tab shows how much of each place's income comes from outside payroll wages."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a Lorenz curve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A chart that lines people up from lowest to highest pay and shows how much of the total pay has been reached at each point. If everyone earned the same, it would be a straight diagonal line. The more it sags below that line, the more unequal the pay."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a Gini coefficient?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A single number for the size of the gap, from 0 to 1. Zero means everyone is paid the same. Here it measures gaps between occupations' average pay among payroll workers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is the Gini lower than numbers I have seen for the U.S.?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Those figures usually measure all income, for households or individuals. This one covers payroll wages only, and it compares occupations' average pay, so differences within an occupation and income from outside payroll are not included. In our view, for payroll wages compared this way, 0.30 is a large gap and 0.20 is a small one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does this chart show that inequality is falling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Over the long run, it shows the gap in payroll wages between occupations narrowing: in 49 of 51 states since 2013, and in 229 of 305 metro areas since 2016. Since 2022 the picture is mixed. Among states, 15 narrowed, 11 widened and 25 show no clear change. Among 307 metro areas, 108 narrowed, 56 widened and 143 show no clear change. Either way, it does not show whether overall inequality fell, because it leaves out business and investment income. Over the same years, the share of U.S. personal income that comes from dividends, interest and rent rose from 18.2% to 21.0%."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which city has the biggest wage gap?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "By the Gini of payroll wages across occupations, San Jose-Sunnyvale-Santa Clara, CA had the largest gap of the 393 metro areas in May 2025 (0.3321), followed by New York-Newark-Jersey City, NY-NJ (0.3171) and Bridgeport-Stamford-Danbury, CT (0.3161). Grants Pass, OR had the smallest (0.2003), not counting Puerto Rico."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which state has the most wage inequality?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "New York, by the Gini of payroll wages across occupations (0.3084 in May 2025), followed by Georgia and Texas. By the pay needed to be in the top 10% compared with the median wage, California is first (173%), then New York (164%). Maine has the smallest gap by the Gini (0.2308), and North Dakota by the top 10% measure (97%)."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is wage inequality getting worse?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Measured as the gap in payroll wages between occupations, no over the long run: it narrowed in 49 of 51 states since 2013 and in 229 of 305 metro areas since 2016. Since 2022 the picture is mixed, with 11 states and 56 metro areas widening. Comparing one May to the next says little, because neighboring BLS estimates share most of their survey data, so we compare only years three apart. None of this covers business or investment income, where the highest incomes are."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why can't I see change over time for some places?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Metro area boundaries changed in 2024. If more than 2% of a metro area's jobs are in counties that were added or removed, we do not compare it across years. Nonmetro areas were redrawn in 2018 and 2024, so they show May 2025 only. The chart says why for each place."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does the home health aide bubble jump in California?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "BLS's count of home health and personal care aides in California rose 221% between May 2016 and May 2017, very likely because caregivers paid through the state's In-Home Supportive Services program began to be counted. Part of that bubble's growth in California is a counting change. Our verdicts for California hold with that group left out."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are the dollars adjusted for inflation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. They are in the dollars of each year. The Gini, the curve and the shares are not affected, because they are ratios."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where does the data come from?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics' Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics survey, May 2013 to May 2025, and the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis's personal income by county, 2013 to 2024."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often is the chart updated?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Once a year, after BLS releases the next May estimates in the spring."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I embed the chart?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. It is free to use and embed. Add #embed=1 to the end of the full visualization's address for the 780-pixel version shown on this page."
          }
        }
      ]
